cellularBinomialIsPrimary -- test for primaryness of a binomial ideal
*********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        cellularBinomialIsPrimary I
  * Inputs:
      * I, a binomial ideal
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note CellVariables: CellVariables, => ..., default value null,
        cellular variables
      * *note ReturnPChars: ReturnPChars, => ..., default value false, return
        two partial characters
      * *note ReturnPrimes: ReturnPrimes, => ..., default value false, return
        two associated primes
  * Outputs:
      * true or false, depending on whether I is a binomial primary ideal

Description
===========

A binomial ideal is primary only if it is cellular. If the cellular variables
are known they can be given via the *note CellVariables: CellVariables, option.
If the ideal is not primary, either 'false' or two distinct associated primes
can be returned. The behaviour can be changed using the options *note
ReturnPrimes: ReturnPrimes, and *note ReturnPChars: ReturnPChars,.
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y]                                           |
|                                                           |
|o1 = R                                                     |
|                                                           |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(x^2-1)                                      |
|                                                           |
|            2                                              |
|o2 = ideal(x  - 1)                                         |
|                                                           |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : cellularBinomialIsPrimary (I,ReturnPrimes=>true)      |
|The radical is not prime, as the character is not saturated|
|                                                           |
|o3 = {ideal (x - 1, x - 1), ideal(x + 1)}                  |
|                                                           |
|o3 : List                                                  |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note cellularBinomialIsPrimary: cellularBinomialIsPrimary, -- test for
    primaryness of a binomial ideal
  * *note CellVariables: CellVariables, -- cellular variables
  * *note ReturnPrimes: ReturnPrimes, -- return two associated primes
  * *note ReturnPChars: ReturnPChars, -- return two partial characters

Ways to use cellularBinomialIsPrimary:
======================================

  * "cellularBinomialIsPrimary(Ideal)"

For the programmer
==================

